detection	Render detection log	2026-10-20T12:38:55.5131372Z [engine] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VRzQNcn2U81VMp3D2bcWfh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\"items\":[{\"type\":\"add_labels\",\"item_number\":18,\"labels\":[\"T: style\",\"C: target version\"]},{\"type\":\"add_comment\",\"body\":\"Thanks for the report! This is an automated first-pass triage; a maintainer will make the final call.\\n\\n**Reproduction:** Black 26.3.1 and 26.5.1 with `--target-version py314`: not confirmed. The triage sandbox runs Python 3.12, which can't parse py314 output, so the safety check failed before any formatting result could be compared.\\n**Assessment:** This looks like deliberate behaviour rather than a bug. Black's `remove_parens_around_except_types` feature removes the parentheses around multiple exception types in `except` and `except*` without `as`, and only for py314 targets. It follows PEP 758, which allows `except A, B:` without parentheses on Python 3.14 and later. The `SyntaxError: multiple exception types must be parenthesized` message you quote would be expected on Python 3.13 or older, but not on 3.14. The feature is listed in `docs/the_black_code_style/future_style.md` (preview features) in this checkout, so I couldn't check how it is classified in 26.x.\\n**Next step:** Please check that the Python that produced the `SyntaxError` is really 3.14 (`python --version`). If it is and 3.14 still rejects the output, please share the exact `python -c` command you ran. If you want to keep the parentheses, target `py313` or lower.\\n\\n<sub>black-issue-triage skill v1.0.0 · suggested for maintainers: `R: not a bug`</sub>\",\"item_number\":\"18\",\"temporary_id\":\"aw_SzvcR15R\"}],\"errors\":[]}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0c896f7a-e1b4-4340-8181-9be465bd82b9","uuid":"35665a2a-c1ae-4529-a375-99d374e99c51","timestamp":"2026-10-20T12:38:51.430Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/gh-aw/threat-detection/agent_output.json","content":"{\"items\":[{\"type\":\"add_labels\",\"item_number\":18,\"labels\":[\"T: style\",\"C: target version\"]},{\"type\":\"add_comment\",\"body\":\"Thanks for the report! This is an automated first-pass triage; a maintainer will make the final call.\\n\\n**Reproduction:** Black 26.3.1 and 26.5.1 with `--target-version py314`: not confirmed. The triage sandbox runs Python 3.12, which can't parse py314 output, so the safety check failed before any formatting result could be compared.\\n**Assessment:** This looks like deliberate behaviour rather than a bug. Black's `remove_parens_around_except_types` feature removes the parentheses around multiple exception types in `except` and `except*` without `as`, and only for py314 targets. It follows PEP 758, which allows `except A, B:` without parentheses on Python 3.14 and later. The `SyntaxError: multiple exception types must be parenthesized` message you quote would be expected on Python 3.13 or older, but not on 3.14. The feature is listed in `docs/the_black_code_style/future_style.md` (preview features) in this checkout, so I couldn't check how it is classified in 26.x.\\n**Next step:** Please check that the Python that produced the `SyntaxError` is really 3.14 (`python --version`). If it is and 3.14 still rejects the output, please share the exact `python -c` command you ran. If you want to keep the parentheses, target `py313` or lower.\\n\\n<sub>black-issue-triage skill v1.0.0 · suggested for maintainers: `R: not a bug`</sub>\",\"item_number\":\"18\",\"temporary_id\":\"aw_SzvcR15R\"}],\"errors\":[]}","numLines":1,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VRzQNcn2U81VMp3D2bcWfh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
